<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Kōgon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1338–1374</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Go-Kōgon was the fourth emperor of the Northern Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, reigning from 1352 to 1371. A son of Emperor Kōgon, he was enthroned by the Ashikaga after Southern forces carried off the senior members of the Northern imperial family, and he was crowned without the imperial regalia. He abdicated in favour of his son Go-En'yū and continued to direct the court as retired emperor until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-kogon.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Kōgon" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Go-K%C5%8Dgon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emperor Go-Kōgon, whose personal name was Iyahito, was born on 23 March 1338. He was the second son of Emperor Kōgon of the Northern Court and the brother of his predecessor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-suko">Emperor Sukō</a>. His mother was Hideko, a daughter of Sanjō Kinhide. His name means the Later Kōgon, and some older sources call him Emperor Kōgon the second. He had many children by several consorts, among them the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-enyu">Emperor Go-En'yū</a>, and several of his sons became imperial prince priests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1351 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> briefly returned to the allegiance of the Southern Dynasty, and the Southern Court briefly consolidated control of the imperial line. The peace fell apart in April 1352, when the Southern Court abducted the retired Northern emperors Kōgon and Kōmyō, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-suko">Emperor Sukō</a> and the Crown Prince Tadahito from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to Anau, the seat of the Southern Court. No emperor remained in Kyoto, and Prince Iyahito became emperor in 1352 with the support of Ashikaga Yoshiakira. Pre-Meiji scholars date his reign from 1352 to 1371, and Go-Kōgon and those around him believed he held the throne from 25 September 1352 to 9 April 1371.</p><p>Public order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> was disturbed by the antagonism between the two courts. The Southern Court repeatedly recaptured Kyoto, and Go-Kōgon was forced to flee several times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a> and other places. Around the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> was named shōgun in 1368, the Southern Court's power weakened and order returned to Kyoto, though the emperor's own authority began to show its weakness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 9 April 1371 Go-Kōgon abdicated in favour of his son, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-enyu">Emperor Go-En'yū</a>. He continued to rule as cloistered emperor until he died of illness on 12 March 1374. He is enshrined with other emperors at the imperial tomb Fukakusa no kita no misasagi in Fushimi-ku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. His reign is dated under several era names, among them Kannō, Bunna, Embun, Kōan, Jōji and Ōan in the Northern Court's reckoning. A Meiji rescript later determined that the Southern Court had been the legitimate one, so the Northern emperors are counted as pretenders; Go-Kōgon's rivals there were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-murakami">Emperor Go-Murakami</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-chokei">Emperor Chōkei</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Go-Kōgon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its emperors included Komyo, Suko, <b>Go-Kogon</b>, Go-En'yu and Go-Komatsu.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a></li></ul></section>
